{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a table.</b> It has already been explained that the Ark was a great necessity, because the tablets of the testimony were placed within it; and the cover (כפורת) as well, for it is the covering that is over it, as in (Genesis 6:14) ״וכפרת אותה״ — which Targum renders ותחפי יתה (and you shall cover it); and the cherubim, which were of the cover, to indicate the existence of angels. But as for the table — what was its function, and what need was there in the Tabernacle and the Temple to make a table in them?" "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the table was required in the House of HaShem because of the bread that was upon it, to be a root-thing through which blessing would flow. And the bread upon it was eaten by the priests, the ministers of the Temple, and a small amount of it sufficed for many of them, as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: every priest who received his portion was satisfied. And therefore it is called a table (שלחן), because the Holy One, blessed be He, sends (שולח) His blessing into the bread that is upon it, and from there the blessing extends to all foods, and satiety comes to all the world. And this blessing was something from something (יש מיש), like the deeds of the prophets and their blessings, which were something from something — for they have no power to make something from nothing (יש מאין). And the proof of this is Elisha in the matter of (II Kings 4:2) ״אסוך שמ״״ — and also Elijah in the matter of (I Kings 17:14) ״כד הקמח״. But the creation of something from nothing is not given into the hand of flesh and blood; and we have not found this after the Creation, except in the creation of the world, as the deed of HaShem, for He is awesome." "<b>And know</b> that the length of the table was twelve handbreadths, and its width six, and its height ten, including the border-frame (מסגרת). And it was placed with its length along the length of the house and its width along the width of the house; thus did the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expound in the chapter 'Shetei ha-Lechem' (Mishnah Menahot 11)." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״ועשית שלחן עצי שטי״ם״ — ש׳ לום ט׳ובה י׳שועה מ׳חילה (shalom, tovah, yeshu'ah, mechilah: peace, good, salvation, forgiveness). And so we find with the Ark and with the altar, for all these good things are included in them and come about through their cause — and also through the cause of the table, for it is an altar of atonement for a person, and the bread upon the table is regarded like a sacrifice upon the altar, when a person is generous of eye and does good and feeds the poor there. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״והמזבח עץ שלש אמות וגו״ (Ezekiel 41:22), ״וידבר אלי זה השלחן אשר לפני ה'״ — it begins with the altar and concludes with the table. That is: when the Temple stands, a person obtains atonement through the altar; now that the Temple does not stand, a person obtains atonement through the table." "<b>And the custom</b> of the pietists of France is to make their table into a coffin for burial, to indicate that a person carries nothing away in his hand, and nothing of his toil remains with him — except the charity he did in his lifetime and the good he bestowed at his table. And therefore the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: one who prolongs [grace] at his table — his days and years are prolonged for him."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:27.053747737Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}